      "content": "Certainly, let's continue with the subgrade point. A subgrade that is not properly compacted can also lead to differential settlement over time. As the pavement experiences repeated thermal cycles, the subgrade may continue to settle unevenly, exacerbating the stress on the asphalt layer. This can lead to the formation of longitudinal cracks as the pavement is unable to accommodate the movement without fracturing.\n\nNow, regarding the binder's fatigue resistance, the binder in asphalt pavements is subjected to a variety of stresses due to thermal cycling. As the temperature fluctuates, the binder expands and contracts, which can lead to fatigue over time. Fatigue resistance is a measure of how well the binder can withstand these repeated stress cycles without cracking. A binder with low fatigue resistance will crack more easily under the influence of thermal expansion and contraction.\n\nThe fatigue resistance of the binder is influenced by its composition and the properties of the asphalt mix. For instance, binders with a higher content of polymers or those that are modified with additives can exhibit improved fatigue resistance. This is because these modifications can enhance the binder's ability to accommodate the stresses induced by thermal cycling.\n\nIn terms of mix design, if the binder's fatigue resistance is found to be inadequate, adjustments may be necessary. This",
